          "details_html": "<ul><li><strong>Change audited:<\/strong> Portfolio issue-admission, consolidation architecture, and dashboard scope accounting<\/li><li><strong>Scope:<\/strong> Project-wide issue-admission review of the 198-proposal post-APPT baseline, including every active area, developed receiving proposals and vehicles, inactive-record preservation, public navigation and area-page formatting, registry classifications, GitHub issue wrappers, Project cards, the Review Ready planning denominator, and the dashboard distinction between audit-earned attainment and administrative scope adjudication.<\/li><li><strong>Score/rebaseline effect:<\/strong> No Proposal Quality Score, rubric version, Review Ready determination, or <strong>Runs<\/strong> count changed. The review merged 65 additional proposal records and retired RIGHTS-004, reducing the active portfolio from 143 after the first consolidation batch to 77, while preserving 27 proposals that independently reached Review Ready through score-bearing audits. Administrative dispositions receive no Review Ready or velocity credit. Targeted Change Audits for FUND-001, IMM-001, JUD-001, JUD-005, REG-001, FED-003, and RIGHTS-004 preserved their existing scores and audit counts.<\/li><li><strong>Findings and corrections:<\/strong> Adopted one proposal per coherent institutional weakness rather than one per manifestation, safeguard, remedy component, or cross-area application; preserved 127 merged and 2 retired records to explain numbering and prior decisions; retained distinct remedies where legal authority, implementing institutions, or abuse safeguards materially differ; kept OVS-008 and REG-002/003/006 independent; retained PRESS-006 as a press-specific anti-retaliation framework coordinated with RET-001; retired the omnibus RIGHTS-004 vehicle while preserving its system-wide reader-navigation function through the later <a href=\"../../topics/immigration-system-reform.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Immigration System Reform<\/a> topic page and routing its substantive defects to narrower proposals or candidates; kept HOR-027 integrated and monitored; and preserved DOJ, ELEC, and WAR without further consolidation. Standardized all area pages on bulleted active and historical lists, linked each complete identifier-and-title label when a standalone page exists, and omitted volatile Project fields from those indexes. Retitled A-11 as <strong>Executive Impoundment and Departure from Congressional Spending Decisions<\/strong> and clarified that its institutional defect is executive implementation of policy beyond or contrary to enacted spending decisions through delay, conditioning, redirection, or nonspending. The working portfolio is now 77 active proposals, with 27 Review Ready and 50 remaining. The dashboard now requires both a ready-level Project status and a score of at least 75, excludes <code>Completed within scope<\/code> and other administrative dispositions, labels 35.1 percent as current active-portfolio coverage rather than earned progress, reports scope contraction separately, and permits scope changes to affect only current-scope coverage and workload planning—not the Review Ready numerator or audit-attainment velocity. The resulting current-scope planning pace is approximately 2.08 additional proposals per week through December 31, 2026, assuming no additions or restorations.<\/li><\/ul>",
